# tri45: the same divided-edge treatment the half-polygon shelf got, applied to the 45-45-90 family
# Started 2026-08-18. AL: "Ship them."
#
# WHY THIS SHELF NEEDS IT. The tri45 shelf carries three palettes and they do not all have the problem:
#
#   tri45sq   S=1, H=sqrt2                 no length is a sum of the others (1+1 = 2, which is not a
#                                          declared length; sqrt2 is not a sum) => edge-to-edge is the
#                                          WHOLE story here, and k<=4 stays exactly as it shipped.
#   tri45two  S=1, H=sqrt2, D=2            D = S + S. The big triangle's hypotenuse can be met by two
#   tri45all  S=1, H=sqrt2, D=2            small triangles' legs. Both of these shipped k<=4 counting
#                                          EDGE-TO-EDGE tilings only.
#
# So two of the three palettes are in the same position the half-polygon boards were in on 2026-08-16,
# and the fix is the same: give the divisible edge a flat 180-degree corner at its midpoint and let the
# ordinary search find both kinds. Measured on 2026-08-17 at k<=3: tri45all goes 1,309 -> 2,301 solver
# solutions, and all 1,309 are still there (exact congruence, board by board).
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS, and it is the same trade AL just ruled on for the half-polygons: divided edges make
# one geometric tiling wear many combinatorial labellings, so the split run reaches a lower k. Anything
# the split palette cannot reach does NOT get topped up from the plain run.

